# `hardhat-upgrades` writes one manifest per network under .openzeppelin/. The named
# networks (sepolia, mainnet) are COMMITTED — they are the storage-layout baseline
# `validateUpgrade` grades a new implementation against. The `unknown-<chainId>.json`
# files are throwaway: every in-process fork run and every ephemeral node writes one.
.openzeppelin/unknown-*.json

# `scripts/deploy-apebond.js` writes the timelock batch it built beside deployments.json,
# so the operator can drive the same operation by hand with `lp-timelock.js`. It is a run
# artifact, not a record: the addresses it names are already in deployments.json.
apebond-*-batch.json

# `scripts/apebond-rehearsal.js` writes the purchase it made beside deployments.json, so the
# claim phase can run in another shell on another day. Same reason as the batch file above:
# it is a run artifact of a TEST-STACK rehearsal, not a record of the deployment.
apebond-rehearsal-*.json

# `scripts/apebond-sign-authorization.js` writes the pair it signed beside deployments.json. It is a
# hand-over artifact for a third-party router on a TEST STACK, not a record of the deployment.
apebond-authorization-*.json
